    "title": "pandas.Index.is_floating — pandas 2.1.4 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.is_floating#\n\n- \n*final* Index.is_floating()[source]#\n- Check if the Index is a floating type. Deprecated since version 2.0.0: Use pandas.api.types.is_float_dtype instead The Index may consist of only floats, NaNs, or a mix of floats, integers, or NaNs. \n  - Returns:\n    - bool\n    - Whether or not the Index only consists of only consists of floats, NaNs, or a mix of floats, integers, or NaNs.\n See also \n  - `is_boolean`\n  - Check if the Index only consists of booleans (deprecated).\n  - `is_integer`\n  - Check if the Index only consists of integers (deprecated).\n  - `is_numeric`\n  - Check if the Index only consists of numeric data (deprecated).\n  - `is_object`\n  - Check if the Index is of the object dtype. (deprecated).\n  - `is_categorical`\n  - Check if the Index holds categorical data (deprecated).\n  - `is_interval`\n  - Check if the Index holds Interval objects (deprecated).\n Examples >>> idx = pd.Index([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]) >>> idx.is_floating() True >>> idx = pd.Index([1.0, 2.0, np.nan, 4.0]) >>> idx.is_floating() True >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4, np.nan]) >>> idx.is_floating() True >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4]) >>> idx.is_floating() False",
    "markdown_length": 1180,
